# Plan: expose operational tunables in /etc/postWolf/config (issue #6a)

## Status

- Not from `README-mqc-issues.md`.  Side track raised during
  issue #6 review: the existing `/etc/postWolf/config` file
  carries URLs but no tuning knobs, and several operational
  constants are presently hardcoded.
- Independent of issues #1–#6.  No wire-format or behavior
  change in the protocol; purely a knob-exposure refactor.
- No security-critical defaults change.  Anything that *would*
  weaken the deployment if tuned wrong stays compiled-in (suite
  IDs, frame format, AAD bytes, signature labels, transcript
  construction).

## Why this exists

postWolf's `socket-level-wrapper-MQC/config.h` already wraps
deployment-tunable constants in `#ifndef` so a downstream builder
can override them at compile time with `-DMQC_…=…`.  The
**runtime** equivalent — `/etc/postWolf/config`, parsed via
Augeas in `mtc-keymaster/read-config/` — currently only carries
URLs (`url-bootstrap`, `url-server`, `url-local`).  An operator
who needs to retune a handshake timeout or a rate-limit bucket
today has to recompile.

This plan moves the operational subset of `config.h` constants
(plus a few that today live only in `mqc.c`) under the same
runtime config, with the compiled-in values as fallback.

## Knobs to expose

Categorized by where they live today and whether they're safe to
expose:

### Already in `config.h` (build-time), promote to runtime

| Key in `config.server` | `config.h` constant | Default | Why expose |
|---|---|---|---|
| `mqc-handshake-stall-sec`     | `MQC_HANDSHAKE_STALL_SEC`     | 3       | per-read socket timeout; bump for very slow links |
| `mqc-handshake-total-sec`     | `MQC_HANDSHAKE_TOTAL_SEC`     | 5       | total handshake wall-clock budget; same |
| `mqc-revoked-cache-ttl-sec`   | `MQC_REVOKED_CACHE_TTL_SEC`   | 86400   | compliance / emergency-rotation drills |
| `mqc-sig-freshness-sec`       | `MQC_SIG_FRESHNESS_SEC`       | 300     | widen for legacy clock-skew issues |

### Currently hardcoded in `mqc.c`, expose

| Key in `config.server` | `mqc.c` constant | Default | Why expose |
|---|---|---|---|
| `mqc-rl-connect-per-min`      | `MQC_RL_CONNECT_MIN`          | 100     | per-deployment threat model |
| `mqc-rl-connect-per-hour`     | `MQC_RL_CONNECT_HOUR`         | 1000    | same |
| `mqc-rl-fail-per-min`         | `MQC_RL_FAIL_MIN`             | 10      | brute-force budget |
| `mqc-rl-fail-per-hour`        | `MQC_RL_FAIL_HOUR`            | 100     | same |
| `mqc-max-msg-bytes`           | `MQC_MAX_MSG`                 | 1048576 | spec §11.3: MAY lower, MUST NOT raise; expose lowering only |
| `mqc-max-handshake-bytes`     | `MQC_MAX_HANDSHAKE`           | 131072  | same |

### Explicitly NOT exposed

The protocol invariants — every `#define` introduced by issues
#1–#5 (`MQC_PROTOCOL_VERSION`, `MQC_SUITE_STRING`,
`MQC_HANDSHAKE_LABEL`, `MQC_AAD_LABEL`, the HKDF info strings,
the frame-type values, the role tags) — stay compiled-in.  They
are not tunables; they're protocol constants whose only legitimate
change is a coordinated version bump under a flag-day cutover
(see [`README-plans.md`](./README-plans.md) "Deployment model").

Exposing any of these would either be a no-op (the value is fixed
at build time anyway, and a peer with a different value can't
interoperate) or a security footgun (e.g., letting an operator
swap the suite string by hand without code review).

### Resolution precedence

For each runtime knob:

1. If `/etc/postWolf/config` has the key under `[global]`, use it.
2. Else use the `#define` from `config.h` (which itself respects
   build-time `-D` overrides).

The compiled-in `#define` is always the floor: if a config-file
value parses as out-of-range (negative, zero, or beyond a
sanity-check ceiling), log a warning and fall back to the
compiled default.

## `config.server` example after this plan

```
[global]

url-bootstrap     https://factsorlie.com:8445
url-server        https://factsorlie.com:8446
url-local         https://factsorlie.com:8444

# --- MQC operational tuning (optional; defaults in comments) ---
# Handshake timeouts (seconds)
mqc-handshake-stall-sec       3
mqc-handshake-total-sec       5

# Frame-size ceilings (bytes; spec §11.3 — MAY lower, MUST NOT raise)
mqc-max-handshake-bytes       131072
mqc-max-msg-bytes             1048576

# Rate-limit buckets (per source IP; Redis-backed)
mqc-rl-connect-per-min        100
mqc-rl-connect-per-hour       1000
mqc-rl-fail-per-min           10
mqc-rl-fail-per-hour          100

# Cache + skew windows (seconds)
mqc-revoked-cache-ttl-sec     86400
mqc-sig-freshness-sec         300
```

All ten knobs are optional; omitting a key leaves the compiled-in
default in force.  A misspelled key is silently ignored (Augeas
returns "not found" → fall back).

## Code edits

### `mtc-keymaster/read-config/read-config.h`

Add an integer accessor parallel to the existing `read_config_url`:

```c
/* Read [global] <key> from /etc/postWolf/config and parse as a
 * positive integer.  Returns the value, or default_val if the
 * key is absent or unparseable.  Logs the source to stderr. */
long read_config_long(const char *key, long default_val);
```

(Names match the existing `read_config_url` style.)

### `mtc-keymaster/read-config/read-config.c`

```c
long read_config_long(const char *key, long default_val)
{
    if (init_augeas() != 0)
        return default_val;
    char *raw = get_augeas(key);
    close_augeas();
    if (!raw)
        return default_val;

    char *end = NULL;
    errno = 0;
    long v = strtol(raw, &end, 10);
    if (errno || end == raw || *end != '\0' || v <= 0) {
        fprintf(stderr,
                "[%s] config /etc/postWolf/config: invalid value %s, "
                "using default %ld\n", key, raw, default_val);
        free(raw);
        return default_val;
    }
    fprintf(stderr,
            "[%s] config /etc/postWolf/config: %ld\n", key, v);
    free(raw);
    return v;
}
```

No changes to `myconf.aug` — the existing IniFile lens accepts
any `key value` pair; per-key typing happens in C.

### `socket-level-wrapper-MQC/mqc.c` — runtime cfg cache

Add a small one-shot loader and a cached struct.  All the
per-key `read_config_long` calls happen exactly once per process
on first need; no per-frame Augeas overhead.

```c
struct mqc_runtime_cfg {
    int initialized;
    long handshake_stall_sec;
    long handshake_total_sec;
    long max_msg_bytes;
    long max_handshake_bytes;
    long rl_connect_per_min;
    long rl_connect_per_hour;
    long rl_fail_per_min;
    long rl_fail_per_hour;
    long revoked_cache_ttl_sec;
    long sig_freshness_sec;
};

static struct mqc_runtime_cfg s_rt_cfg;
static pthread_once_t          s_rt_cfg_once = PTHREAD_ONCE_INIT;

static void mqc_rt_cfg_init_once(void)
{
    s_rt_cfg.handshake_stall_sec =
        read_config_long("global/mqc-handshake-stall-sec",
                         MQC_HANDSHAKE_STALL_SEC);
    s_rt_cfg.handshake_total_sec =
        read_config_long("global/mqc-handshake-total-sec",
                         MQC_HANDSHAKE_TOTAL_SEC);
    s_rt_cfg.max_msg_bytes =
        read_config_long("global/mqc-max-msg-bytes",
                         MQC_MAX_MSG);
    s_rt_cfg.max_handshake_bytes =
        read_config_long("global/mqc-max-handshake-bytes",
                         MQC_MAX_HANDSHAKE);
    s_rt_cfg.rl_connect_per_min =
        read_config_long("global/mqc-rl-connect-per-min",
                         MQC_RL_CONNECT_MIN);
    /* ... and so on for the other six ... */

    /* Sanity ceilings: if config asks for something insane, clamp. */
    if (s_rt_cfg.max_msg_bytes > MQC_MAX_MSG)
        s_rt_cfg.max_msg_bytes = MQC_MAX_MSG;          /* spec §11.3 */
    if (s_rt_cfg.max_handshake_bytes > MQC_MAX_HANDSHAKE)
        s_rt_cfg.max_handshake_bytes = MQC_MAX_HANDSHAKE;

    s_rt_cfg.initialized = 1;
}

static const struct mqc_runtime_cfg *mqc_rt_cfg(void)
{
    pthread_once(&s_rt_cfg_once, mqc_rt_cfg_init_once);
    return &s_rt_cfg;
}
```

The clamp on `max_msg_bytes` / `max_handshake_bytes` enforces the
spec rule "MAY lower, MUST NOT raise" automatically: an operator
who tries to raise the ceiling silently gets the compiled-in cap.
This is the right failure mode (no peer in the wild speaks a
larger frame; raising would just produce frames the peer rejects)
and it doesn't require any extra moving parts.

### `socket-level-wrapper-MQC/mqc.c` — call-site updates

Replace each compiled-in reference with the cached lookup:

| Line | Today | After |
|---|---|---|
| 380 | `count_m > MQC_RL_CONNECT_MIN`     | `count_m > mqc_rt_cfg()->rl_connect_per_min` |
| 385 | `count_h > MQC_RL_CONNECT_HOUR`    | `count_h > mqc_rt_cfg()->rl_connect_per_hour` |
| 411 | `count_m >= MQC_RL_FAIL_MIN \|\| …` | `… mqc_rt_cfg()->rl_fail_per_min …` |
| 934, 1447, 1671 | `set_socket_timeout(fd, MQC_HANDSHAKE_TIMEOUT)` | `set_socket_timeout(fd, mqc_rt_cfg()->handshake_stall_sec)` |
| 945, 1159, 2020 | `MQC_MAX_HANDSHAKE` / `MQC_MAX_MSG` | cached values |

The handshake-total-sec budget is enforced via the
`HANDSHAKE_DEADLINE_ACTIVE()` macro defined elsewhere in
`mqc.c`; that macro reads `MQC_HANDSHAKE_TOTAL_SEC` once.  Update
the macro to read the cached cfg.

### Client-side: revoked cache TTL

`socket-level-wrapper-MQC/mqc_peer.c` (or wherever
`MQC_REVOKED_CACHE_TTL_SEC` is consumed) gets the same
`mqc_rt_cfg()->revoked_cache_ttl_sec` substitution.

### Server-side: signature freshness

Wherever `MQC_SIG_FRESHNESS_SEC` is enforced (in MTC handlers
like `handle_revoke` per the comment in `config.h`), the same
cached lookup applies.  These call sites are in
`mtc-keymaster/server2/c/` — touching them is allowed for this
plan because the constant *originates* in `config.h` (shared
between MQC and MTC) and the runtime override should apply
uniformly.  Specifically: `mtc_http.c` and any handler that
gates signature timestamps.  Identify call sites with
`grep -rn MQC_SIG_FRESHNESS_SEC mtc-keymaster/server2/c/`.

### Linkage

`socket-level-wrapper-MQC/Makefile` already builds `libmqc.a`
from `mqc.c` + `mqc_peer.c`.  Add the read-config translation
unit to the link, or expose `read_config_long` from a shared
library that both `libmqc.a` and `mtc_server` already link
against.  Cleanest path: move `read-config.c` into a small
`libreadconfig.a` (or fold into `libpostWolf.so` since that's
already linked everywhere) and link `libmqc.a` against it.
Picking which is a Makefile question, not a security one; either
works.

## Spec edits (`draft-page-mqc-protocol-00.md`)

§11 (Operational Parameters) — extend the existing prose:

> Implementations on Linux SHOULD honor runtime overrides of the
> following parameters via `/etc/postWolf/config` under the
> `[global]` section: `mqc-handshake-stall-sec`,
> `mqc-handshake-total-sec`, `mqc-max-msg-bytes`,
> `mqc-max-handshake-bytes`, `mqc-rl-connect-per-min`,
> `mqc-rl-connect-per-hour`, `mqc-rl-fail-per-min`,
> `mqc-rl-fail-per-hour`, `mqc-revoked-cache-ttl-sec`,
> `mqc-sig-freshness-sec`.  The compiled-in defaults from
> §11.1, §11.2, §11.3, §10.5 apply when the keys are absent.
> Operators MAY lower frame-size ceilings; raising them above
> the §11.3 hard limits has no effect (the ceiling is clamped
> at parse time).

This is informative, not normative — Linux-specific paths and
Augeas-based parsing aren't a protocol property.  Implementations
on other platforms get to pick their own runtime-config
mechanism.

## Tests

Live in `mtc-keymaster/read-config/` (the existing test harness
already has `TEST_MAIN`-style C tests for `read-config.c`):

1. **read-config-int-happy** — write a temp config file with
   `mqc-handshake-stall-sec 7`, call `read_config_long`, assert
   returns 7.
2. **read-config-int-default** — temp config with no such key,
   `read_config_long(…, 3)` returns 3.
3. **read-config-int-malformed** — temp config with
   `mqc-handshake-stall-sec abc`, returns the default and logs a
   warning.
4. **read-config-int-negative** — `mqc-handshake-stall-sec -1`,
   returns default + warning.

Plus an end-to-end smoke under `socket-level-wrapper-MQC/examples/`:

5. **rt-cfg-handshake-timeout** — set
   `mqc-handshake-stall-sec 1` in `/etc/postWolf/config` (use a
   tmpdir + `MQC_CONFIG_PATH` env override during the test —
   `read-config.c`'s hardcoded `/etc/postWolf/config` may need a
   minor tweak to honor an env override for testing; that tweak
   is part of this plan).  Open a TCP connection to the MQC
   port, sleep 2 s before sending the first byte, verify the
   server kills the connection inside 1.5 s.
6. **rt-cfg-frame-cap-clamp** — set `mqc-max-msg-bytes
   2147483647` (overflow attempt), assert the resolved value is
   clamped to `MQC_MAX_MSG = 1048576`.

## Migration runbook (factsorlie.com)

Per the deployment-model rule in `README-plans.md`, no flag-day
required — this plan is wire-quiet *and* behavior-conservative
(absent config keys = today's behavior).

1. Build + install: `./make-all.sh`.
2. Optional: edit `/etc/postWolf/config` to add any keys an
   operator wants to override.  Keys absent → defaults preserved.
3. `sudo systemctl restart mtc-ca.service`.
4. `journalctl -u mtc-ca.service -f` should show the
   `read_config_long` "[<key>] config …" lines for any keys
   that were set; no lines for keys that weren't.
5. Smoke test: `echo hi | mqc --encode --env --no-cache | mqc
   --decode --env --no-cache` → `hi`.

A v0 → upgraded server with no config-file changes behaves
identically to a pre-issue-6a server.  This is the only plan in
the series that's safe to deploy without coordinating any
client-side changes.

## Verification

1. **Zero-warning build** (CLAUDE.md guardrail).
2. **`make check`** clean (existing tests still pass; six new
   ones — four in `read-config/`, two in MQC examples — also
   pass).
3. **Service health**: `systemctl is-active mtc-ca.service` →
   `active`.
4. **Default behavior preserved**: with empty
   `/etc/postWolf/config` MQC section, `journalctl` shows no
   per-key config lines and the timeouts/limits are exactly
   today's values.
5. **Override observable**: with one knob set in the config file,
   the new value shows up in `journalctl` at process start and
   the corresponding behavior changes (e.g., `MQC_SECURITY
   "RATE_LIMITED"` line at the new threshold).

## Critical files to modify

Ranked by depth of change:

1. `mtc-keymaster/read-config/read-config.c` and
   `read-config.h` — add `read_config_long`, optional
   `MQC_CONFIG_PATH` env override for tests.
2. `socket-level-wrapper-MQC/mqc.c` — `mqc_runtime_cfg`
   struct, one-shot loader, ten call-site updates, the
   `HANDSHAKE_DEADLINE_ACTIVE()` macro update.
3. `socket-level-wrapper-MQC/mqc_peer.c` — revoked-cache TTL
   call-site update.
4. `mtc-keymaster/server2/c/mtc_http.c` (and any other handler
   that enforces `MQC_SIG_FRESHNESS_SEC`) — call-site update.
5. `socket-level-wrapper-MQC/Makefile` and/or
   `mtc-keymaster/server2/c/Makefile` — link `read-config.o`
   into `libmqc.a` or its consumer.
6. `mtc-keymaster/read-config/config.server` — operator example
   showing the ten new keys (commented out, so the file's
   semantics don't change without operator action).
7. `socket-level-wrapper-MQC/draft-page-mqc-protocol-00.md` —
   §11 paragraph (informative).

## Out of scope (deliberately deferred)

- **Protocol invariants exposed as tunables.**  Suite IDs, label
  strings, AAD format, frame types, version number, HKDF info
  strings, transcript construction — none of these become
  runtime knobs.  Per the deployment-model rule, the only way
  to change them is a code edit + flag-day cutover.
- **Live config reload (SIGHUP).**  The plan loads config once
  per process at startup.  Operators who need to retune after
  a deploy restart the service (`systemctl restart
  mtc-ca.service`).  SIGHUP-based reloading would require
  rwlocks around `s_rt_cfg`; that complexity isn't justified
  for the typical "edit config, restart service" workflow.
- **Per-client (vs per-server) config**.  The plan exposes the
  same keys to client tools (which read the same
  `/etc/postWolf/config`).  A client-only knob (e.g.,
  per-tool revoked-cache TTL override) is a separate change.
- **Dynamic suite negotiation**, exporter labels, PSK
  resumption parameters — none exist in MQC v0; nothing to
  expose.
- **Other postWolf libraries (SLC, MQCP) tunables.**  This
  plan is MQC-only.  The same pattern (Augeas-backed runtime
  override of a `config.h` `#define`) generalizes to SLC's
  `socket-level-wrapper/config.h` and MQCP's
  `socket-level-wrapper-QUIC/config.h`, but those are
  separate plans.
